{"schema": "workere-benchmark-suite/v1", "protocol": "workere-historical-relations/v1", "config": {"seed": 42, "active_dataset": "causal_timebank", "datasets": {"causal_timebank": {"name": "CausalTimeBank-standard-eci", "repo_id": "Nofing/CausalTimeBank-standard-eci-workere", "split": "train", "text_field": "text", "ann_field": "annots", "max_examples": 0, "kfold": {"enabled": true, "n_folds": 10}, "constrain_to_pair_list": false}}, "data": {"shuffle_docs": true, "shuffle_pair_list": true, "reshuffle_per_resample": true, "binary_undirected": false, "vote_to_binary": false, "binary_default": "norel", "novote_norel": true, "labels": ["causes", "causedby", "norel"]}, "experiment": {"resampling": {"enabled": true, "n_runs": 7, "tie_breaking": "norel", "distinct_fewshots": true}}}, "documents": [{"id": "NYT19980424.0421", "idx": 31, "text": "The New York Times <ei1 said> in an editorial on Saturday , April 25 : The Supreme Court <ei3 took> a detour this week from the core principle of gender fairness it <ei5 vindicated> two years ago in its <ei7 ruling> <ei8 invalidating> the <ei9 use> of sexual stereotypes to justify denying women admission to the Virginia Military Institute . By a 6-3 vote , the court <ei10 upheld> a discriminatory immigration law that gives a child born overseas to an unmarried American woman a better chance at citizenship than a child born to an unmarried American man . Under the statute , a child born abroad to an unwed American mother is automatically considered a U.S. citizen . But the child of an unwed American father has no citizenship rights unless the father takes the affirmative step of acknowledging paternity and formally agrees to provide support until the child turns 18 . The majority 's views were <ei11 expressed> in three separate opinions , the most disquieting of which was <ei12 written> by a liberal member , Justice John Paul Stevens , and <ei13 joined> by a conservative , Chief Justice William Rehnquist . The <ei14 opinion> , which <ei15 found> the gender distinction reasonable , <ei16 relied> heavily on outmoded <ei17 generalities> about family roles , including the flawed <ei18 presumption> that mothers have a closer relationship to their children than fathers do . As Justice Stephen Breyer <ei19 asked> in a strong <ei20 dissent> , \" what sense does it make \" to apply citizenship barriers \" only to fathers and not to mothers in today 's world _ where paternity can readily be proved and where women and men both are likely to earn a living in the workplace ? \" . Justices Sandra Day O ' Connor and Anthony Kennedy seemed to <ei21 agree> that the law <ei22 amounted> to unconstitutional sex discrimination . Butinstead of <ei23 providing> the votes to <ei24 strike> it down , they <ei25 chose> to <ei26 uphold> it on the flimsy ground that because the sex of the parent and not the child made the difference under the law , the plaintiff did not have standing to <ei27 bring> the case . The Justice Department , which <ei28 supported> the statute , did not <ei29 cover> itself with glory either . The department <ei30 retreated> from its position in the VMI case when it <ei31 argued> that the government could not <ei32 make> <ei33 policy> based on stereotypes \" even when those stereotypes <ei34 reflect> current realities . \" As for the court , it <ei36 managed> with its splintered ruling to <ei37 cast> doubt both on the constitutionality of the immigration provisions it upheld and on its true commitment to fighting gender stereotypes .", "gold": [["ei17", "causes", "ei14"], ["ei18", "causes", "ei14"], ["ei14", "causedby", "ei17"], ["ei14", "causedby", "ei18"]], "lang": "eng", "mentions": ["ei1", "ei3", "ei5", "ei7", "ei8", "ei9", "ei10", "ei11", "ei12", "ei13", "ei14", "ei15", "ei16", "ei17", "ei18", "ei19", "ei20", "ei21", "ei22", "ei23", "ei24", "ei25", "ei26", "ei27", "ei28", "ei29", "ei30", "ei31", "ei32", "ei33", "ei34", "ei36", "ei37"], "surfaces": {"ei1": "said", "ei3": "took", "ei5": "vindicat